{
  "verdict": "divergent",
  "divergences": [
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "description": "Reinhardt’s factivity axiom is `K φ → φ`, but `Def11.k3` asserts its converse, `φ → K φ`. The latter lets one infer `K φ` even when `φ` is merely a hypothesis in `T`. The passage’s discussion of the rule `p / Bp` does not justify this object-language implication or supply the missing factivity axiom."
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "`Def12` allows any universal prefix, including one that leaves variables free or binds variables vacuously. Thus `Def13` also admits `K ψ` when `ψ` is still open, whereas Reinhardt specifies universal closures and speaks of prefixing `B` to the resulting sentences."
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "`Def14.hyp` accepts open formulas as premises. Reinhardt defines deduction from a set `T` of sentences; Lean includes that case but does not restrict `T` to it."
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "As declared in the claim, the relational fragment has only variables as terms, so instantiation is variable-for-variable. It cannot state the cited equality axiom (Leibniz’s law) or the arithmetic schemas, including induction in the expanded language."
    }
  ],
  "summary": "Within the declared fragment, Lean captures modus ponens and three of Reinhardt’s four special axioms. Reversing factivity changes the proof system materially, independently of the broader treatment of open formulas."
}
